routes.brief:
This reuses the candidate proposed by PAPER-LI-ZHANG-22-B and extended by PAPER-LI-LIU-22 (exotic smooth ramified case). He–Li–Shi–Yang, Invent. Math. 234 (2023), 721–817, add the Krämer (semistable) ramified case: Int(L) = ∂Den(L), where ∂Den is Den′ corrected by c_{2j}-multiples of local densities against duals of vertex lattices.

Import:
- the Krämer model N_n and its hermitian space from UnitaryRapoportZinkSpacesAndRSZModels;
- ramified local densities, the primitive decomposition and Theorem 7.1 from GeometryOfNumbersAndQuadraticArithmetic GN.3;
- local Fourier analysis from AutomorphicLFunctionsAndLocalFactors AL.0;
- K-theory with supports from SchemeKTheoryOperations S.6–S.7.

Construct:
- special cycles and the canonical lift;
- the exceptional divisors Exc_Λ ≅ P^{n−1};
- horizontal and vertical parts, derived special cycles and Int(L) (proper, finite);
- the Deligne–Lusztig varieties S_V, Y_V and the minuscule cycles N_Λ ≅ Y_V (smooth projective of dimension t(Λ)/2), with the deformation-theoretic Lemma 3.8 and Propositions 3.10, 3.12;
- the Bruhat–Tits stratification (Theorem 3.19, Proposition 3.20 for integral L; sourceIssues E2);
- horizontal lattices and the primitive horizontal cycles Z̃(M^♭)° (from Shi, He–Shi–Yang);
- the modified cycles ^𝕃Z(L^♭)^*_{H/V} (Definitions 4.5, 4.7);
- the spaces S(V)_{≥m}.

The final theorems:
- Proposition 2.12, the geometric cancellation law, with Int(I_n) = δ_odd(n);
- Theorems 4.14–4.16, the geometric vertical part and its partial Fourier transform, constant on W_{≥0};
- Theorem 8.2, the analytic vertical part, constant on W_{≥0} \ {0} and zero on W_{<0};
- the horizontal identity (Theorem 9.5) and the induction (Theorem 9.7), hence Theorem 1.3;
- globally, the modified central derivative ∂Eis and Theorems 10.1–10.2.

Keep p odd. Theorem 10.1 at almost self-dual inert places uses Li–Zhang Theorem 10.5.1, conditional on Li–Zhang Conjecture 10.4.1 (sourceIssues E1). Import it once Li–Rapoport–Zhang (arXiv:2404.02214) Theorem 14.6.2 is checked to supply that conjecture, or keep it as a named hypothesis; the ramified places do not depend on it.

Tests:
- n = 2 against Shi and He–Shi–Yang;
- Int(I_n) = δ_odd(n);
- the identity (4.9) in Lemma 4.9;
- the cancellation law against Corollary 7.3;
- Theorem 1.3 for n = 3 against He–Shi–Yang.

This reuses the candidate proposed by PAPER-LI-ZHANG-22-B and extended by PAPER-LI-LIU-22 and PAPER-LI-LIU-21; keep it moduli-theoretic.

Add the Krämer level at a ramified place:
- formal hermitian O_F-modules;
- the Krämer model N_n with Krämer's filtration condition, representable, flat of relative dimension n − 1, regular and semistable (Howard 2019, Prop. 2.2; Mihatsch's relative RZ spaces), with its two framing objects;
- the hermitian spaces C and V with the isometry b : V → C of (2.5)–(2.6);
- globally, the RSZ Shimura datum G̃ = Z^Q × Res U(V) and the regular semistable models M_K under (G0)–(G5) and (H1)–(H4) (RSZ20 Thms. 5.2 and 5.4, RSZ21 Def. 6.10).

The deformation theory used by the cycle roadmap is relative O_{F_0}-displays (Ahsendorf–Cheng–Zink), already required by this Part II. Keep p odd.

Tests:
- n = 1, 2;
- the two framing objects isomorphic for n odd, non-isomorphic for n even.

routes.reason:
GN.3 constructs local representation densities. This source adds:
- the ramified hermitian densities against unimodular lattices I_n and duals of vertex lattices;
- the primitive decomposition (Lemma 5.3, Theorem 5.5, Theorem 6.1) and the finite-field isometry and subspace counts it reduces to (§5.3);
- the closed formula of Theorem 7.1 for the modified derivative of primitive densities, with its consequences (integrality, the cancellation law) and the polynomial identities of §7;
- the μ-invariants of full-type lattices (Propositions 8.7, 8.9) and the valuation-reduction Lemma 9.6.
The q-binomial theorem itself is planned in QSeriesPartitionsAndMockModularForms QM.0.
